< In Penrose's Conformal Cyclic Cosmology (CCC) the future conformal boundary $\scri$ of one aeon is identified with the initial singularity of the next, and the rescaled matter fields are required to pass through it. We study a test Dirac field of mass $m$ on de Sitter space and on spatially flat FLRW backgrounds that reach $\scri$ in finite conformal time, rescaled with the standard conformal weight $3/2$. (i) Every non-zero mode keeps a constant norm and acquires the phase $\exp(\pm\ii\int m\,\dd t)$, so that the rescaled field has no limit at $\scri$; this asymptotic form is known, and we read it at $\scri$. In conformal time the mode equation has a regular singular point at $\scri$ with exponents $\pm\ii m/H$. (ii) For solutions that contain, at $\scri$, only one of the two exponents (late-time particle or late-time antiparticle solutions), the winding is a common phase which a $U(1)$ gauge transformation of a charged field removes, at the price of a Maxwell potential with a non-integrable pole; for solutions that contain both, the relative phase is gauge invariant. There is no gauge in which both the rescaled field and the potential extend continuously. (iii) With the radiation weight $\Omega^{-4}$, the rescaled energy density behaves as $m(\|\chi_{0}^{+}\|^{2}-\|\chi_{0}^{-}\|^{2})/\Omega$; the coefficient has no fixed sign and may vanish. With the dust weight $\Omega^{-3}$ used by Friedrich for pressure-free matter the same density is regular: the pole belongs to the choice of variable, while the interference between the two exponents does not. For a one-particle excitation of a pure quasi-free Hadamard state the statement holds for the expectation value relative to the state, integrated over a comoving slice. (iv) For a time-dependent mass we give three strictly ordered conditions ($\int^{\infty}m\,\dd t<\infty$; $m\,a$ bounded; $m=\Omega\mu$ with $\mu$ smooth). We state the consequences for CCC as conditions on a chosen matching rule, not as an exclusion of every matching.
