< \paragraph{Related work.} The mode functions of the massive Dirac field in the flat chart of de Sitter space are Hankel functions of order $\tfrac12\mp\ii m/H$ \cite{Cotaescu2002}. Their late-time form, a plane wave $e^{\mp\ii mt}$ in each eigenspace of $\gamma^{0}$ plus an exponentially small error, is Lemma~3.1 of Dappiaggi, Finster, Murro and Radici \cite{DFMR2019} (after Finster and Reintjes \cite{FinsterReintjes2016}); Ibison \cite{Ibison2011} describes the infinitely fast phase oscillation at the future conformal boundary and the growth, in the conformal frame, of the rest energy of a free electron. The fermionic pair-creation factor $1/(e^{2\pi m/H}+1)$ is standard \cite{Boonpan2026,BirrellDavies}. For a massive scalar field in CCC, Bachelot \cite{Bachelot2020} gives integral conditions on a decaying mass under which the rescaled field crosses the bang surface; Friedrich \cite{Friedrich2015} shows that the coupled Einstein--massive-scalar system is regular at $\scri$ when $m^{2}=2\lambda/3$, and notes that each massive field needs its own analysis; for Einstein--$\lambda$--dust he constructs an open set of solutions with a smooth $\scri$ \cite{Friedrich2017}. Conformal scattering has been developed for the massless Dirac field on Kerr space-time \cite{Pham2021}; see also \cite{MasonNicolas2004} on conformal scattering and \cite{MasonNicolas2012} on the peeling of Dirac and Maxwell fields on a Schwarzschild background; the conformal extension of the Bunch--Davies state across the de Sitter boundary has been studied for the scalar field \cite{Wrochna2017}. On the CCC side, the asymptotic decay of rest mass is an assumption that recent work does not address \cite{MarkwellStevens2022}, and Penrose's ``suppressed rest-mass hypothesis'' is a condition on the conformal factor \cite{GurzadyanPenrose2013,MarkwellStevens2022}. Proposition~\ref{prop:dirac} below is therefore a reading at $\scri$ of a known asymptotic form; the classical core of Proposition~\ref{prop:energy} is in \cite{Ibison2011}; Proposition~\ref{prop:varmass} is the Dirac counterpart of \cite{Bachelot2020}, with a different exponent; we have not found Proposition~\ref{prop:gauge}. The search is not exhaustive and we make no priority claim.
