< Let $f$ be a function of $\eta$ on $(\eta_{1},0)$. We say that $f$ \emph{has a limit} (or extends \emph{continuously}) if $\lim_{\eta\to0^{-}}f$ exists; that it is \emph{bounded} or \emph{integrable} if $\sup|f|<\infty$ or $\int^{0}|f|\,\dd\eta<\infty$; that it extends \emph{smoothly} if it is the restriction of a $C^{\infty}$ function on a neighbourhood of $\eta=0$. These notions are distinct: $\sin(\ln|\eta|)$ is bounded without limit, $|\eta|^{-1/2}$ is integrable and unbounded, $|\eta|^{1/2}$ is continuous and not smooth.
